"06_listview.mst — the ListView display-details path (DD11).

 WHAT WAS WRONG, proven by counters (CLAUDE.md rule 4): every one of the
 ListView's ~168 LVN_GETDISPINFO callbacks raised on its FIRST send —
 `anLVITEM handle`, `subclassResponsibility` from CCITEM — because
 `OS.LVITEMW` was never in the translator's `--reopen` list, so the
 corpus's item protocol (`handle`, `textPointerOffset`, `iStateImage:`,
 ...) never arrived and the generated struct inherited only CCITEM's
 abstract stubs. Counter run before: `Dictionary (#lv_enter->169)` —
 nothing past the first statement; after the reopen the text branch
 completed (`#lv_text->89`) and the image branch named the next hole.
 The reopen itself is the fix (tools/translate_mvp.py now lists
 OS.LVITEMW beside the TVITEMW pair); what remains in THIS file are the
 defects the emitted reopen carries plus two base-image gaps the newly
 reachable code exposed, each probed on the construction path (rule 3)
 before it could fail silently in a window."

"── the corpus-vs-winkb field rename: RETIRED before it merged ──────────
 The corpus names LVITEMW's state field `dwState`; winkb names it `state`.
 This file briefly carried a hand alias (`LVITEMW class >> _OffsetOf_dwState
 [ ^self _OffsetOf_state ]`) — written concurrently with the tree-expansion
 work that hit the SAME rename on TVITEMW and closed it as a CLASS:
 `genstructs` now collects the corpus's declared constant names,
 de-Hungarianises them, and emits alias constants into the generated structs
 (see `st/prims/structs/LVITEMW.mst` line ~25, the corpus-alias line).
 The hand alias was dropped at merge review: a duplicate of a generated
 method is a silent override waiting for the generator to change. Note
 `#lv_state->80` in the counter runs — the state branch is exercised on the
 demo path, so the alias is load-bearing, not defensive."

"── allCallbacks: an unfolded QUALIFIED pool constant ───────────────────
 The corpus writes `OS.Win32Constants.LPSTR_TEXTCALLBACK` fully
 qualified, and the reopen emitter folds only bare pool names — so the
 emitted method read `put: Win32Constants.LPSTR_TEXTCALLBACK`, which this
 dialect parses as `put: Win32Constants` (an unbound global, so NIL)
 followed by two no-op statements. Probed as `the method '&' was called
 on null`. Also 32-bit-poisoned in the corpus itself (rule 1):
 LPSTR_TEXTCALLBACK is ((LPWSTR)-1) — POINTER-sized — and the corpus
 writes it with `uint32AtOffset:`, which on this target fills only the
 low half of an 8-byte field. Both constants fold to -1 (I_IMAGECALLBACK,
 and -1 sign-extended over the full pointer via intPtrAtOffset:).
 RETIRED when the emitter folds qualified pool references."
LVITEMW extend [
    allCallbacks [
        | b |
        b := self bytes.
        b int32AtOffset: LVITEMW _OffsetOf_iImage put: -1.
        b intPtrAtOffset: LVITEMW _OffsetOf_pszText put: -1.
        self maskIn: 3
    ]
]

"── callbacksForIndex: — a 32-BIT prototype over a missing idiom ────────
 Dolphin holds `CallbackPrototype` in its image, so the corpus carries it
 as a 60-byte LITERAL — LVITEMW's Win32 size, with the pszText callback
 marker at the Win32 offset (20; it is 24-wide-8 here). Rule 1 in its
 purest form: every byte of it is a 32-bit assumption. Here it also loads
 as a plain List (`Class 'List' has no instance method
 'int32AtOffset_put_'`), and the method's `basicNew bytes:` construction
 is an idiom this port has no equivalent for (`bytes` answers the
 receiver; there is no `bytes:` — probed as `does not understand
 bytes:`). Rebuilt FRESH per call instead: same result — an 88-byte item
 in full callback mode with iItem set — by the corpus's own `allCallbacks`
 recipe, with no prototype to get stale. The emitted
 `initializeClassConstants` still assigns the dead 60-byte List to
 `CallbackPrototype`; nothing reads it once this override is in place.
 RETIRED if the emitter learns to re-derive prototype constants instead
 of copying their bytes."
LVITEMW extend [
    LVITEMW class >> callbacksForIndex: anInteger [
        | item |
        item := self new.
        item allCallbacks.
        item iItem: anInteger.
        ^item
    ]
]

"── row:text:imageIndex:indent: — a mangled `??` ────────────────────────
 The corpus writes `imageInteger ?? UI.ListView.NoImageIndex`, and the
 emitter's `??` rewrite cannot carry a QUALIFIED right-hand side: it
 emitted `(imageInteger ifNil: [ UI ]) .ListView.NoImageIndex`, which
 this dialect parses as `iImage := imageInteger ifNil: [UI]` followed by
 two bare-global statements — so a nil imageIndex silently became the
 class UI. Not on the virtual-mode demo path (which inserts by count, not
 by item), fixed because the static update mode reaches it on its first
 use. RETIRED when the emitter's `??` rewrite handles qualified names."
LVITEMW extend [
    row: rowInteger text: aString imageIndex: imageInteger indent: indentInteger [
        | mask iImage |
        self iItem: rowInteger - 1.
        self pszText: (text := aString asUtf16String).
        mask := 3.
        iImage := imageInteger ifNil: [ ListView classVarNoImageIndex ].
        indentInteger == 0
            ifFalse: [
                self iIndent: indentInteger.
                mask := mask bitOr: 16.
                indentInteger < 0 ifTrue: [ iImage := ListView classVarNoImageIndex ] ].
        self iImage: iImage - 1.
        self mask: mask
    ]
]

"── `??` exists nowhere in this image ───────────────────────────────────
 Four generated methods (ListView>>onDisplayDetailsRequired:,
 TreeViewStaticUpdateMode, ListViewColumn, TreeView) carry a LITERAL
 `?? ` send — the emitter rewrites most of the corpus's `??` to `ifNil:`
 but misses these (and refuses 3 more, counted as `qq` in its report).
 The parser accepts the selector, so the files load and the send raises
 only when REACHED: probed as `Class 'int' has no instance method '??'`.
 Defined with Dolphin's semantics — answer the receiver unless it is nil,
 else the operand; the operand is always evaluated, exactly as a binary
 send must. Not in the IL builder's rewrite table (checked via
 tools/audit_helpers.py), so a Smalltalk definition is LIVE. RETIRED when
 the emitter's `??` rewrite covers every form and the literal sends stop
 being emitted."
Object extend [
    ?? anOperand [ ^self ]
]
UndefinedObject extend [
    ?? anOperand [ ^anOperand ]
]

"── no per-row icons until the icon machinery lands ─────────────────────
 The port-wide default getImageBlock is
 `Message selector: #iconImageIndex` (View class), so in virtual mode
 EVERY image request runs `rowObject icon` -> `self class icon` ->
 `^##(self defaultIcon)`. `defaultIcon` is defined in Dolphin's BASE
 IMAGE, outside the corpus slice this port translates — it exists
 NOWHERE here, so all 80 image-request callbacks in a two-second run
 raised `stClassSend: 'String ext' has no class-side method
 'defaultIcon'` (counters isolated it to `imageFromRow:`:
 `#lv_img_recv->80`, `#lv_img_fromrow` absent; the door named the send).
 And behind `defaultIcon` sits the rest of the chain — `Icon>>extent` is
 `^initializer extent` over GetIconInfo, then `addToImageList:mask:`
 over real HICONs — none of it translated.

 A STAND-IN at the shallowest honest point: a nil getImageBlock is
 Dolphin's own first-class 'list without images' state
 (`imageFromRow:` answers -1 by design), so the PORT's default is
 no-images. The TreeView is unaffected — its image branch is gated by
 `hasIcons`, false at its #noIcons default viewMode, which is why the
 tree's callbacks completed while the list's raised. RETIRED when the
 icon-resource machinery (defaultIcon, Icon>>extent, ImageList
 population) is translated — restore Dolphin's default by deleting this
 override.

 NOTE for that day: the corpus's `icon` is `^##(self defaultIcon)` —
 compile-time bound so every class WITHOUT an override answers OBJECT's
 default icon. The hashhash lowering re-evaluates `[self defaultIcon]
 value` per send, so a class-side `defaultIcon` override WILL be
 honoured where Dolphin deliberately ignores it. Recorded so it is not
 rediscovered the hard way."
View extend [
    View class >> defaultGetImageBlock [ ^nil ]
]
